lifeos-nix patch f4-e — privacy: unregister the Atlas event-hint hook (Atlas off means off)

PROBLEM
  f4-d turned the Atlas surface off (PULSE.toml atlas = false, so pulse.ts:311 never
  imports the module) and hard-killed the Insights narrative. But hooks/hooks.json still
  registered AtlasEventCapture.hook.ts on four PostToolUse matchers (Write, Edit,
  MultiEdit, Bash), so the hint-PRODUCING half of Atlas kept running with no consumer:
  every matching tool call appended a line to ~/.local/state/lifeos/atlas/events.jsonl,
  a file that only `atlas tick` reads — and nothing installs the tick unit.

  Observed live before this patch: 6 lines written in one session, all `source:cloudflare`,
  fired by the matcher regex hitting COMMAND TEXT that merely mentioned deploys. The hints
  carry only {ts, source, tool} — no command text, no data, and they never leave the
  machine — so this was never a leak. It was a half-wired subsystem: an append-forever file
  feeding a consumer that does not exist, and a live component of something we call off.

  Half-wired states are precisely what produced the Atlas problem in the first place (a
  module flag on, its data source never installed). "Off" should mean off in one piece.

KILLED HERE
  hooks/hooks.json — all four AtlasEventCapture registrations removed. The now-empty
  PostToolUse "Bash" matcher group is dropped too, rather than left as `"hooks": []`
  dead config. Pure deletion: 25 lines out, 0 in, no reformat of the rest of the file.

WHY A PATCH AND NOT THE USER OVERLAY
  MergeSettings composes LIFEOS/USER/CONFIG/hooks.user.json over the official base by
  UNION per event (MergeSettings.ts ~line 648: `[...composed[event], ...entries]`). The
  overlay can only ADD entries, never remove one, so an official registration can only be
  dropped in the official file — i.e. here. This also makes the fix cover fresh installs
  of this flake, not just the maintainer's live tree, which is what Klemen asked for.

DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED
  - hooks/AtlasEventCapture.hook.ts stays in the tree, unregistered and inert. Keeping the
    file costs nothing and keeps the patch to config only; re-registering is a one-line
    overlay entry the day Atlas is deliberately turned on.
  - The Atlas CLI, Store, collectors and systemd templates: local, no egress, untouched.

NO NEW TAB EITHER (already true, restated so it is not re-litigated)
  Pulse gates the /atlas page solely on `config.modules.atlas` (pulse.ts:311) — there is
  no separate nav/tier list carrying the tab. f4-d's `atlas = false` therefore already
  means fresh installs of this flake get no Atlas tab at all, not a tab that 404s.

DECISION PROVENANCE
  Klemen, 2026-09-01: "daj ugasni tudi ta hook ja, lepo prosim, in naredi tudi da ne bo
  taba z Atlasom, tudi za nove uporabnike, oboje." Companion decision from the same thread:
  do NOT install the Atlas tick unit even dormant — a local SQLite map of the whole estate
  that nothing reads is cost without benefit, and the remaining exposure is the assistant
  reading it into context, not the code. Install it when Atlas is deliberately adopted.

VERIFICATION
  Post-build: `grep -c AtlasEventCapture hooks/hooks.json` = 0; the file still parses as
  JSON and every other hook registration is byte-identical. Post-deploy on the live tree:
  no new lines in ~/.local/state/lifeos/atlas/events.jsonl across a session of tool calls.
  Hunks generated by `git diff` against 7.40.4 with f4-c, f-derived-watch-config and f4-d
  already applied (never hand-counted).

